sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch06-07> delphi:hasAssessment <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-ch06-07--grok> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-ch06-07--grok> a delphi:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Grok 4.7 on thompson1917-ch06-07: needs-new-data, resolvability 0.40" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/dataset-resolvability-0.2.0> ;
    delphi:assessmentType "dataset-resolvability/0.2.0" ;
    delphi:hasScore "0.4"^^xsd:double ;
    delphi:hasVerdict "needs-new-data" ;
    delphi:hasConfidence "0.6"^^xsd:double ;
    dct:description "Coincidence of a potassium peak with the conjugation papilla or the spore outgrowth is a concrete spatial prediction. No current public dataset repeats that histochemistry with coordinates and time. The test is straightforward in principle and cannot be run on data that exist today." ;
    rdfs:comment "Proposed test with existing data: Map potassium through conjugation in zygnematacean filaments and through rhizoid outgrowth of Equisetum spores. A potassium peak that precedes the bulge and disperses when symmetry returns would support the claim; outgrowths without a potassium peak, or peaks that persist after the cell is symmetrical, would refute it." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/agent/delphisci-resolvability-assessor-grok-4.7-high-fast> ;
    delphi:evidenceAgainst "The spatial potassium records for Pleurocarpus, Mesocarpus and Equisetum spores are essentially early histochemical plates, not reusable measurements.", "A spatial coincidence, even if remeasured, would still leave open whether potassium causes the bulge or follows local growth." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-ch06-07--grok--operationalizability> a delphi:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Operationalizability of thompson1917-ch06-07 by Grok 4.7: 0.8" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/dataset-resolvability-0.2.0-operationalizability> ;
